 ### [Hybrid Updates for Editing](15974-hybrid-updates-for-editing.md)
-Hybrid Updates for Editing is a strategy that targets specific network components for updates to balance efficiency and accuracy during user interaction in conditional radiance fields. It updates only specific layers of the network and latent codes to achieve both accuracy and efficiency, avoiding the slowness of updating all weights and the low quality of updating only latent codes. Unlike updating the entire network or only codes, this method updates only a subset of components (later layers) to achieve significant computational speed-up.
+Hybrid Updates for Editing targets specific network components for updates to balance efficiency and accuracy during user interaction in conditional radiance fields. It updates only specific layers and latent codes, avoiding the slowness of updating all weights and the low quality of updating only latent codes. Unlike updating the entire network or only codes, this method updates only a subset of components (later layers) to achieve significant computational speed-up.
 
 ### [Conditional Radiance Field Editing](15977-conditional-radiance-field-editing.md)
+Conditional Radiance Field Editing modifies a trained conditional radiance field to alter its appearance or geometry without retraining the entire model from scratch. It solves the problem of efficient, targeted scene manipulation by freezing the coarse network and optimizing only the fine network's parameters and associated latent codes. Unlike Hybrid Updates for Editing, which may involve more complex update strategies, this method relies on a strict separation of coarse and fine networks to isolate the editing domain.
+
+### [Network with Shared Branch](15980-network-with-shared-branch.md)
+A modular conditional radiance field architecture that incorporates a shape branch shared across object instances to capture part semantics and improve shape reconstruction accuracy. It sets itself apart from sibling methods like CodeNeRF, which learns separate embeddings for each instance, by enforcing an inductive bias that encourages a common representation across different objects of the same class. This shared structure enhances shape editing consistency and generalization.
+
+### [GSN Model Architecture Components](16659-gsn-model-architecture-components.md)
+GSN decomposes a scene into many independent locally conditioned radiance fields to improve generative performance over global conditioning methods like GRAF. It distinguishes itself by using modulated linear layers in the generator and a local latent code representation $W \in \mathbb{R}^{c \times s \times s}$, whereas GRAF uses global conditioning with standard linear layers and $\pi$-GAN uses modulated sine layers.
+
+### [Generative Vertex-based Radiance Fields](19577-generative-vertex-based-radiance-fields.md)
+VeRi3D is a generative radiance field method that maps 3D points to color and density using the $K$ nearest SMPL vertices and their local coordinate systems, enabling free control over camera pose, human pose, shape, and part-level editing. It differs from surface-based warping methods (which suffer from ghosting artifacts due to inaccurate mapping) and blend-weight-field methods (which struggle to generalize to out-of-distribution poses) by using fixed SMPL skinning weights and individual local coordinate systems for each point.
 
 ### Locally Conditioned Radiance Field Network
-A neural radiance field network that maps 3D coordinates and view directions to appearance $a$ and occupancy $\sigma$, conditioned on local latent codes $w_{ij}$ to represent multiple distinct radiance fields [S2].
+A neural radiance field network that maps 3D coordinates and view directions to appearance $a$ and occupancy $\sigma$, conditioned on local latent codes $w_{ij}$ to represent multiple distinct radiance fields [S2]. It conditions each modulated linear layer with $w_{ij}$, where each modulated linear layer has 128 channels [S2]. During volumetric rendering, it thresholds occupancy values $\sigma$ using a softplus function instead of a standard ReLU [S2]. After rendering, it upsamples the resulting feature map with refinement blocks until the desired resolution is achieved, then applies a sigmoid activation to bound the final output [S2]. It samples 64 points per ray, and uses feature map sampling resolution of 32×32 for 64×64 images or 64×64 for 128×128 images [S2]. Sampling higher resolution feature maps directly yields higher quality but incurs significantly higher computational cost [S2].
 
-*   **Definition:** A modular network architecture for conditional radiance fields that includes a shape branch shared across object instances to capture part semantics [S1].
-*   **Layout:** The network weights are updated during color and shape editing, while codes remain static in the "Network with Shared Branch" variant [S1]. In the "Hybrid Update" variant, the shape code is updated during shape editing, network weights are updated during color editing, and network weights are updated during shape editing [S1].
-*   **Comparison:** Sampling higher resolution feature maps directly from the radiance field yields higher quality results than sampling at low resolution and applying many refinement blocks, but incurs significantly higher computational cost [S2].
+### Convolutional Shading
+A post-processing step that converts duplex features and view directions to per-pixel colors in image space using a small receptive field CNN [S3]. For each pixel, it identifies neighboring local geometry positions $x_i$ whose projection $K(Rx_i + t)$ lies in a local window around the pixel location, where $R$ is camera pose, $t$ is translation, and $K$ is the intrinsic matrix [S3]. It uses a 2×2 convolution kernel and 2 to 3 convolutional layers [S3]. The CNN is implemented in two rendering passes, one pass for each layer, executed in a pixel-parallel way [S3]. The WebGL implementation uses 8-dimensional features and removes the final convolution layer compared to the CUDA version [S3].
 
-### [GSN Model Architecture Components](16659-gsn-model-architecture-components.md)
-GSN uses locally conditioned radiance fields, where the scene is decomposed into many independent locally conditioned radiance fields, improving performance over global conditioning.
+### Audio-Driven Neural Radiance Fields
+An audio-driven neural radiance field that maps audio features to a conditioning feature for the radiance field MLP, providing explicit control over rigid head pose [S4, unofficial]. It employs two separate radiance fields: one for the head and one for the torso [S4, unofficial].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** The base architecture has similar time complexity to standard NeRF per ray evaluation, but the modular design and code injection add minor overhead. Hybrid updates reduce training time for editing tasks by limiting the number of parameters updated [S1].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** The bottleneck layer (8-dimensional input to color branch) allows for feature caching, reducing memory usage during editing [S1]. Standard NeRF memory usage applies to the forward pass.
